    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1019">

                <pb facs="1019" n="1007" xml:id="pg1007"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94114A/10–2344: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bern</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-23">October 23,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received October 23—11:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7021. American Interests, Far East. Legation’s airgram 484, August 16.<anchor
                        n="82" xml:id="d1019-f1"/> Foreign Office note October 19 states according
                    Japanese Foreign Office beriberi cases do not exceed 1% POWs Japan and barely
                    exceed 2% POWs Thailand and Burma, View action taken these figures tend
                    decline.</p>
                <p>Japanese Foreign Office adds notwithstanding present difficulties, POWs continue
                    receive food equal quantity and quality Japanese base troops and this food even
                    superior that of Japanese civilians.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harrison</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1019-f1" n="82" xml:id="d1019-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that the Swiss Legation in Tokyo had invited the
                    attention of the Japanese Government to the fact that many POWs were dying of
                    beriberi because their diet of polished rice had insufficient vitamins and that
                    the Swiss Legation had been informed by the Japanese Foreign Office that rations
                    accorded POWs had been minutely studied and, as a result, unhusked rice and
                    concentrated vitamins were now being provided (711.94114A/8–1644).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
